  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe Long Term forecast remains relatively unchanged. The center of \nthe upper-level ridge remains east of our area through much of this \nweek. However, because we will be on the western periphery of the \nridge, temperatures stay above normal. Highs in the mid-to-upper 90s \nwill be commonplace Tuesday and Wednesday, with 100s confined mainly \nto the river valleys. By the latter half of the week, the ridge \nstarts to shift westward again, but is dampened by shortwaves moving \nacross northern portions of the CONUS. As a result, temperatures \ntick down a degree or two Thursday, and by Friday most locations top \nout in the low-to-mid 90s. Surface troughing and pulses rounding the \nridge will also yield increasing rain chances across mainly the \nwestern half of the region and in/near the higher terrain. Chances \nfor these areas range from 30-60% in general. Going into the \nweekend, guidance continues to depict a front pushing down towards \nwest Texas and southeast New Mexico. This front would help further \ncool temperatures across the region, and would encourage increasing \nrain chances across a larger portion of the area. However, exact \ndetails regarding the timing of the front, rain chances, and the \nextent of cooling remain uncertain at this time. Given this \nuncertainty, the NBM is justifiably a bit conservative, showing \nhighs in the upper 80s and low 90s for most locations Saturday and \nSunday, which would at least be near climatology for this time of \nyear. We will continue to monitor trends, so stay tuned for updates!\n\nSprang",
